  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Useful as a lower-layer envelope lineage, but it does not itself model route announcements, scope restraint, or leak evidence. It fits this scenario mainly by keeping the carrier/payload boundary explicit and by exploring signature and unknown-pCID design space that later routing-policy proofs would depend on.",
    "strengths": [
      "Clear envelope-versus-payload boundary reduces the chance of treating wire format as routing policy.",
      "Successor variants explicitly explore unknown-pCID and signature-placement choices relevant to later audit evidence.",
      "Non-canonical, specimen-oriented lineage is strong on evolution safety."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No direct representation of restricted transit promises, export scope, or route-leak violations.",
      "Parent simulation is an owner/history node rather than a concrete routing or audit specimen.",
      "Canonical serialization, pCID meaning, and signature binding are still open questions."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "A positive read here could over-credit a generic envelope for solving policy enforcement it does not define.",
      "Unsettled signature and pCID semantics could undermine durable leak evidence."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What payload protocol would encode route-policy scope and onward-restraint promises inside the envelope?",
      "Which child variant, if any, gives the right unknown-pCID and signature behavior for routing-policy auditing?",
      "What peer-local records would Alice, Bob, and Carol keep to prove a route leak without a central registry?",
      "Should any routing metadata appear in pCID, or must policy semantics stay entirely in payloads?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
